 [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Given that the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=- 4 x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03efd4b4-041a-5564-818d-06a45aad1a76" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-4) & =-4(2) \\\\ & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,-4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-8)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,-4,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518873 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Given that the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bc337d9-050a-554a-8d77-360e1bc2538f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-2,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((-2)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(1)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(9) & =(- \\pi - 1)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z & =- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518847 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(3,-2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2 x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b0f9bb8-9802-5c08-ac17-d465c6cdfcd9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,-2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,-2) & =2(3) \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(3,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-2)}(x-(3)) \\\\ z-(3) & =(6)(x-(3)) \\\\ z & =6 x - 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,-2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=6 x - 15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518838 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,-10)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3 x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ed4f78d-95a2-5ea0-b370-b6d2838b98e8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,1,-10)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & =3(2) \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,1)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(-10) & =(6)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =6 x - 22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,-10)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=6 x - 22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518876 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518860 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Given that the point $P(3,- \\pi,-11)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e59d941-71dc-5f4e-89e0-08125c1f2994" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518860 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,- \\pi,-11)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(3,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((3)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(-1.0)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =-1.0 + 1.0 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(3,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,- \\pi)}(x-(3)) \\\\ z-(-11) & =(-1 + \\pi)(x-(3)) \\\\ z & =- x + \\pi x - 3 \\pi - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,- \\pi,-11)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- x + \\pi x - 3 \\pi - 8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518867 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518848 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518867 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518867 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2 \\pi.$ Given that the point $P(3,2 \\pi,12)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "767e873f-9d5e-53b8-a351-ac97e9472597" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518867 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2 \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,2 \\pi,12)$ will be given by the partial derivative 