valuate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,- 3 \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,- 3 \\pi) & =(- 3 \\pi)\\cos((2)(- 3 \\pi))+\\cos(- 3 \\pi) \\\\ & =(- 3 \\pi)(1.0)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- 3.0 \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,- 3 \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,- 3 \\pi)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(5) & =(- 3 \\pi - 1)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =- 3 \\pi x - x + 7 + 6 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,- 3 \\pi,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- 3 \\pi \\\\ z &=- 3 \\pi x - x + 7 + 6 \\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518904 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186124438.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518904 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "004c08d0-a323-5f8e-9da1-7f9ed62ba16b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531746 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531731 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531731 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,-4,6)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y+2\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531731 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01c658f5-4f30-5b39-9d41-0213df71f8bb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531731 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{(-4)}-5(3)^2(-4)+2\\cos(\\pi (-4)) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{15}{4})+(180)+(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{713}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 175921860